{"slug":"what-is-ebitda","short":"What is EBITDA?",
"title":"What Is EBITDA? Simple Definition, Formula and Example",
"h1":"What is EBITDA? Simple definition and example",
"desc":"What EBITDA means, how to calculate it, why bankers and investors use it so much, and its biggest weaknesses, explained simply.",
"related":["valuation-multiples","enterprise-value-vs-equity-value","free-cash-flow-explained"],
"lede":["EBITDA stands for Earnings Before Interest, Taxes, Depreciation and Amortization. It's a quick way to measure how much money a company's core business makes.","It ignores how the company is financed (interest), taxes, and non-cash costs (depreciation and amortization), so it's easier to compare companies."],
"body":[("h2","How to calculate it"),
("card","Formula","EBITDA = Net income + Interest + Taxes + Depreciation + Amortization. Or: Operating income (EBIT) + Depreciation + Amortization.",""),
("table",["Item","Amount"],[["Net income","$50M"],["+ Interest","$10M"],["+ Taxes","$15M"],["+ Depreciation & amortization","$25M"],["= EBITDA","$100M"]]),
("h2","Common questions"),
("qa","Why do investment bankers use EBITDA?","It's a simple way to compare the core profits of different companies, and it's used in the popular EV/EBITDA valuation multiple."),
("qa","What is wrong with EBITDA?","It ignores real costs like buying equipment (capital expenditures) and changes in working capital, so it can make a company look healthier than its actual cash flow."),
("qa","Is EBITDA the same as cash flow?","No. Cash flow also subtracts things like capital expenditures, taxes and working capital changes."),
C]},
{"slug":"wacc-explained","short":"WACC explained",
"title":"WACC Explained Simply: Formula, Example and Interview Questions",
"h1":"WACC explained simply",
"desc":"What WACC (weighted average cost of capital) means, the formula, a worked example, and how it's used in a DCF, at a beginner level.",
"related":["dcf-interview-questions","enterprise-value-vs-equity-value","what-is-ebitda"],
"lede":["WACC is the average return a company has to pay the people who fund it: its lenders (debt) and its owners (equity). It's weighted by how much of each the company uses.","In a DCF, WACC is the discount rate. A higher WACC means future cash is worth less today."],
"body":[("h2","The formula"),
("card","WACC","WACC = (% equity × cost of equity) + (% debt × cost of debt × (1 − tax rate)). Debt gets the tax adjustment because interest is tax-deductible.",""),
("table",["Input","Example"],[["Equity / debt mix","70% / 30%"],["Cost of equity","10%"],["Cost of debt","6%, tax rate 25% → 4.5% after tax"],["WACC","0.7 × 10% + 0.3 × 4.5% = 8.35%"]]),
("h2","Common questions"),
("qa","How do you calculate the cost of equity?","Usually with CAPM: risk-free rate + beta × equity risk premium."),
("qa","Why is the cost of equity higher than the cost of debt?","Shareholders get paid last and take more risk, so they expect a higher return than lenders."),
("qa","What happens to a DCF if WACC goes up?","The company's value goes down, because future cash flows are discounted more."),
C]},
